import OddPerfect.ExcessFourReduction
import OddPerfect.TwoExcEngineTail

/-!
# Total excess at least 4: exclusion S1

Let `N` be an odd perfect number with special prime `q` and exponent 6 at 3, at most one further
prime `r` of exponent 4, every other prime factor except `q` of exponent 2. This module proves
that no such `N` exists (`excessS1 : ExcessS1`, the statement of `ExcessFourReduction`). This
concerns only this restricted family, not odd perfect numbers in general. Whether this result is
new has not been verified against the literature.

## The proof

The proof is a kernel checked certificate for the checker `checkV` of `OddPerfect.TwoExcEngine`,
a tool whose soundness theorem `checkV_sound` is proved in `TwoExcEngineCheck` and
`TwoExcEngineTail` (`defSound`). The certificate `cert_ex_S1_100000_nojump_nolanded.jsonl`
(sha256 `162b09f35a59f3ec665ea177aad7d4b0f7c542388be8cb135831ee0fa56c967f`, 398 lines) is the
output of an exact search. The checker parameters allow 2 exceptional primes (the prime 3 is a
key of exact exponent 6 and counts as one of them), every exception other than 3 of exponent
exactly 4, and do not use the landed facts. As a checker term it has 346 records, 89
contradiction leaves, 29 abundant leaves, 1 deficient leaf, and 227 forced nodes, 1 closed node,
50 decide nodes. The term is checked in one kernel evaluation; primality enters only through the
verified oracle `prS1`, which the checker asks about 59 primes (41 below 4096, covered by the
verified table `primeB`; the others by one flat Pratt certificate of 28 entries and a search
tree over its primes). The bridge `famV_S1` turns the hypotheses of `ExcessS1` into the family
of the parameters.

Generated by `gen_excess.py` from `cert_ex_S1_100000_nojump_nolanded.jsonl`.

Landed theorems used: the tail bound of the deficient leaves, through `defSound` and
`RankinTailGeneral`; nothing else beyond the engine.
-/

open OddPerfect.TwoExcEngine

set_option maxRecDepth 100000

